  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, drawn at height 0.5 × loss. It is 3 times steeper in x, so overshoot starts there: learning rates above 2/3 make x swing wider each step until the run diverges.",
    "Each step: settings −= learning rate × (true slope + noise). Noise is random, centred on zero, with size 1.5·√((64−b)/(63·b)) for batch b: largest at 1, exactly zero at 64.",
    "The orange arrow is the next mini-batch step; the grey arrow is the full-data step (learning rate × true slope, pointing downhill). Both are drawn at 3 times their true length; the readout gives the true length.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished), or when the loss passes 6 (diverged; the ball is then held at the bowl's rim and the arrows are hidden). The trail holds the start plus one point per step.",
    "Noise comes from a fixed seed, so Reset replays the same sequence; a new run continues the sequence, so its noise differs. The ball takes 5 steps per second at 1× speed."
  ]
}